The recoverable amount of the CGU is calculated based on its value in use, which requires management to make highly judgmental assumptions about the future. This presents a significant risk due to error and management bias.
• Assessed the appropriateness of the JLR Group’s key assumptions used in the current discounted cash flow forecasts by comparing those, where appropriate, to historical trends;
In particular, there are execution risks associated with planned profit optimisation, the Group’s transition to Battery Electric Vehicles (‘BEV’) and economic uncertainty resulting from geo-political factors that could impact demand or supply.
• Assessed and critically challenged the appropriateness of the JLR Group’s assumptions used in the cash flow projections by comparing key assumptions to relevant internal and external data sources;
The effect of these matters is that, as part of our risk assessment, we determined that the calculation of the value in use of CGU to which property, plant and equipment, intangible assets, and right-of-use assets are allocated has a high degree of estimation uncertainty, with a potential range of reasonable outcomes greater than our materiality for the financial statements as a whole, and possibly many times that amount.
• Compared the JLR Group’s discount rate and long- term growth rate to external benchmark data and comparative companies;

